5791	The in-memory hot path is now a process-local `DashMap` only:
5792	
5793	```rust
5794	use std::sync::{Arc, OnceLock};
5795	
5796	use camino::Utf8Path;
5797	use dashmap::DashMap;
5798	
5799	use super::file_analysis::FileAnalysis;
5800	
5801	type InMemoryCache = DashMap<String, Arc<FileAnalysis>>;
5802	
5803	fn cache() -> &'static InMemoryCache {
5804	    static CACHE: OnceLock<InMemoryCache> = OnceLock::new();
5805	    CACHE.get_or_init(DashMap::new)
5806	}
5807	
5808	/// Look up a cache entry by path.
5809	///
5810	/// Returns a cloned `Arc` from the cache, or `None` if not present.
5811	pub(crate) fn lookup(path: &Utf8Path) -> Option<Arc<FileAnalysis>> {
5812	    cache()
5813	        .get(path.as_str())
5814	        .map(|entry_ref| Arc::clone(entry_ref.value()))
5815	}
5816	
5817	/// Insert or update the cache with a complete analysis.
5818	///
5819	/// Any existing entry for this path is unconditionally replaced — every
5820	/// `FileAnalysis` is now fully populated, so there is no downgrade risk.
5821	pub(crate) fn insert(path: &Utf8Path, a: Arc<FileAnalysis>) {
5822	    cache().insert(path.to_string(), a);
5823	}
5824	```
5825	
5826	Key change vs. Phase 1: the previous module wrapped a `Mutex<CacheMeta>` around the inner state and used `fs2::FileExt` advisory locks at the cache layer to coordinate cross-process disk writes. Phase 2+ splits responsibilities cleanly:
5827	
5828	- **`analysis/cache.rs`** is now lock-free for reads (DashMap shard locks only), and is process-local.
5829	- **`analysis/persist.rs`** owns disk persistence. It is the layer that uses `Mutex<CacheMeta>` for the per-config-hash metadata and `fs2` advisory locks for the on-disk write coordination (see `RustAnalyzer::analyze_cached_or_parse` calling `persist::lookup`, `persist::lookup_by_hash`, `persist::refresh_mtime`, `persist::queue_write`, and `persist::init`).
5830	
5831	This split is the ADR-0016 boundary referenced in the call-out: hot reads of a current-process analysis don't have to traverse a mutex; cross-process invalidation lives in the disk persistence layer.
